shot shows zero failed services and no unexpected listening sockets outside expected scope. The only diffs in the lifecycle comparison (boot-identity and /tmp systemd-private directories) are natural artifacts of the mandated reboot, not unnecessary mutations. Package health remained normal (dpkg) across both snapshots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b8642be01a404124\",\"cmd-f2e4280d92444564\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task — serving the inventory app on TCP 8181 and keeping AppArmor in enforce mode — are directly and repeatedly verified via ss, curl, and aa-status, both before and after a full native reboot of node1. The independently required node-restart evidence protocol is also satisfied with a clean pre-restart baseline, an observed disconnection during reboot, a changed boot_id, and full recovery of service, port, and MAC state, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. No unnecessary or unsafe mutations were found.\",\"limitations\":[\"No evidence was captured of a live AppArmor denial/audit-log check tied specifically to the inventory-app process (e.g., dmesg or journalctl apparmor entries), so enforcement is confirmed at the system/profile level rather than shown actively mediating the new service; this is expected since AppArmor ships no default profile for python3 http.server, not a gap in the executor's work.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Inventory application served over TCP port 8181 on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"systemd unit inventory-app.service runs `python3 -m http.server 8181` from /opt/inventory-app, verified active with ss showing LISTEN on *:8181 and curl returning HTTP 200 with inventory HTML content, both before and after the node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b8642be01a404124","cmd-f2e4280d92444564","cmd-fa7e15955021443c","cmd-47c5fc4b0d5b49b4"]},